# Expectation record for 05_pointers/007_casts_roundtrip.c
#
# Reproduce one cell by hand with no harness: render the three command templates
# below, substituting the target triple, the optimization level, the source path,
# the output path, and the target's runner (empty on the natively executing target).

program            = 007_casts_roundtrip
area               = 05_pointers
description        = Object-pointer casts round-tripped through a sufficiently wide integer type, asserted only by pointer equality
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
Each round-trip is exact, so every reconstructed pointer designates the same object as the
original and dereferencing it is defined. No cast anywhere in this file crosses a width
boundary, so no value is ever truncated. The null pointer round-trip reconstructs a null
pointer and that pointer is compared but NEVER dereferenced. Every interior pointer used
is inside its array: iarr has 8 elements indexed 0..7 and carr has 16 indexed 0..15. The
char-pointer byte view is formed and converted back but is never used to write, so no
aliasing rule is violated. The const object cobj is read through a const-qualified pointer
and is never written.
No signed integer overflow, no shift out of range, no aliasing violation, no read of
uninitialized storage, no object modified twice between sequence points, at most one
side-effecting argument per call, and no dependence on padding bytes or on the relative
addresses of unrelated objects. The only external symbol is printf; no header is included,
no file, socket, environment variable, clock or random source is touched, and every input
is a literal in this source file.
END
impl_defined_notes <<END
WIDTH REASONING, recorded because it is what makes this program portable across all four
targets. sizeof(void *) is 4 on i686 and 8 on x86-64, AArch64 and RISC-V 64. The carrier
type is unsigned long long, which is 8 bytes on all four targets and is therefore always
sufficiently wide; uintptr_t is deliberately NOT used because it would require stdint.h
and no header may be included. The conversion back to a pointer is bridged through
unsigned long, which is exactly pointer width on all four targets (8 on x86-64, AArch64
and RISC-V 64; 4 on i686), so a direct cast between an 8-byte integer and a 4-byte pointer
never occurs and nothing can be truncated on i686.
Neither the pointer nor the integer carrier is ever printed; the round-trip is asserted
ONLY by comparing the reconstructed pointer for equality with the original, and by reading
the object it designates. The two leading lines assert the two width facts this program
depends on rather than assuming them: both are 1 on all four targets.
No address or pointer value is printed. Pointer facts appear only as differences,
comparisons and alignment residues, and every pointer difference is cast to long long
before printing, so the printed text is identical whether ptrdiff_t is 32 or 64 bits.
Plain char is never printed in a signedness-dependent way. The only conversion
specifiers used anywhere in this program are %d, %lld and fixed-precision %.3f, all of
which are target-width independent.
END
expected_stdout    <<END
carrier_wide_enough=1
bridge_is_pointer_width=1
int_roundtrip_eq=1
